<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kalaburagi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Gulbarga, Ahsanabad</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gulbarga, officially Kalaburagi since 2014, is a city in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah">Alauddin Hasan Bahman Shah</a> made it the first capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> in 1347, and it remained the capital until the court moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> in the 1420s. The Bahmani fort with its great domed mosque, the royal tombs and the shrine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gesu-daraz">Gesu Daraz</a> date from that period.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kalaburagi.jpg" alt="Kalaburagi" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dayal, Deen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Mosque_in_Gulbarga_Fort..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kalaburagi lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, at an elevation of 300 to 750 metres in the district, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> and Bhima rivers cross. The soil is mainly black soil, and the district's main crops are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groundnut">groundnuts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pulses">pulses</a>. The climate is hot and semi-arid, with a rainy season brought by the south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> from late June to late October and dry winters from late November to February.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest recorded name of the region dates from the 6th century, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalukya-dynasty">Chalukyas</a> called it Kalburgi, roughly 'rocky land' in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a>. The Rashtrakutas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yadava">Yadavas</a> and Kakatiyas kept the name until 1321, when the region was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, which renamed it Gulbarga, 'city of flower gardens', from the Persian gul and bagh. The Kakatiya power was subdued in 1308, and the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a> passed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><p>A revolt of the officers appointed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> led to the founding of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> in 1347 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ala-ud-din-bahman-shah">Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah</a>, who chose Gulbarga, also called Hasanabad, as his capital. When the sultanate ended in 1527 it broke into five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-sultanates">Deccan sultanates</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a>, Ahmednagar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a>, and the Gulbarga area was divided between Bidar and Bijapur. Golconda, the last of them, fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> in 1687, and Gulbarga passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p><p>Early in the 18th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asaf-jah-i">Asaf Jah I</a>, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>'s generals, formed the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, which took in most of the area. In 1948 Hyderabad State joined the Indian Union, and in 1956 the Gulbarga district became part of the new Mysore State. The state government restored the name Kalaburagi in 2014.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The city is the headquarters of Kalaburagi district and division. The Jama Masjid inside the fort was built in 1367 by a Persian architect named Rafi, during the reign of the Bahmani king Mohammed Shah I. Other Bahmani monuments include the Haft Gumbaz and the Shor Gumbad, and painted floral, geometric and calligraphic designs decorate the domed tombs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saints and of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-bahmani">Firuz Shah Bahmani</a>. Buildings in the city, with others in the region, were placed on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s tentative list as Monuments and Forts of the Deccan Sultanate in 2014. The 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 533,587 people, of whom 55 per cent gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> as their first language and about 36 per cent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kalaburagi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He soon moved his capital to <b>Gulbarga</b>, which he renamed Ahsanabad, in the heart of the Deccan plateau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bijapur Sultanate was one of five states that emerged from the break-up of the Bahmani Sultanate, which had ruled the Deccan from <b>Gulbarga</b> and later Bidar since 1347.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The capital moved several times: the early kings ruled from the Ellora region, Govinda III from Mayurakhandi, and Amoghavarsha I founded Manyakheta, modern Malkhed in <b>Kalaburagi</b> district, which remained the seat of government until 972.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
